# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sendstack
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# You only need the two values in the first block. Everything else — Resend,
# Redis, Inngest, branding, sign-in methods — is configured in the setup wizard
# the first time you open the app, and stored (encrypted) in your database.
#
# The optional variables further down are SEEDS: if one is set before first
# run, the wizard pre-fills that field. Once a value is saved through the UI,
# the saved value wins and the environment variable is ignored. The Settings
# page labels every field with where its value actually came from.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# REQUIRED — these two cannot live in the database
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Settings stored in Postgres cannot contain the credentials needed to reach
# Postgres, and secrets encrypted at rest cannot contain their own key. So
# these two, and only these two, stay here.
#
# If you have neither yet, start the app anyway — the wizard will collect them,
# test the connection, and write this file for you when the filesystem allows
# it (a VPS, Docker, local development). On a read-only serverless filesystem
# it hands you the exact values to paste into your host instead.

# Any Postgres 14+ — Neon, Supabase, RDS, or `docker compose up`.
# If the host is a connection pooler (Neon's "-pooler", Supabase port 6543, any
# PgBouncer) leave it as is: prepared statements are already disabled, which is
# what pooled connections require.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@host/sendstack?sslmode=require"
#
# Connections the app may hold open at once, per process. Default 10, which
# suits one long-running server. On serverless (Vercel, Lambda) every warm
# function is its own process with its own pool, so 10 becomes 10 × however
# many instances the platform has spun up — enough to exhaust a small Postgres
# on a busy afternoon. Behind a pooler (Neon "-pooler", Supabase 6543,
# PgBouncer) set this to 2–5 and let the pooler do the multiplexing.
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# Generate with:  openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Signs your sessions, encrypts every credential the wizard saves, and signs
# every unsubscribe link you send. Treat it as permanent: changing it signs
# everyone out, makes stored API keys unreadable, and breaks the unsubscribe
# link in every email you have ever sent — those must keep working for years.
AUTH_SECRET="generate-me-with-openssl-rand-base64-32"


# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# OPTIONAL — first-run seeds for the wizard
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Set any of these to pre-fill the corresponding field. Leave them out entirely
# and the wizard simply asks, with instructions for getting each one.

# ── Branding ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SENDSTACK_APP_NAME="Acme Mail"
#
# Where this instance is reachable. Used for auth callbacks, unsubscribe links
# and the logo URL inside your emails, so it must be the public address in
# production. Also settable in the wizard.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL="http://localhost:3000"
#
# Hostnames allowed to request `next dev` internals — HMR, chunks, source maps.
# Needed only when the browser reaches the dev server on some origin other than
# the one it was started from, which is exactly what a tunnel does. Hostnames
# only, no scheme; wildcards allowed. Ignored in production.
# See docs/CLOUDFLARE.md and `pnpm tunnel`.
# ALLOWED_DEV_ORIGINS="mail.example.com"
# ALLOWED_DEV_ORIGINS="*.trycloudflare.com"

# Register the service worker in development too, so a PWA can be installed
# and push tested from a phone against `pnpm dev` + a tunnel. Off by default:
# a worker caching a dev build is an edit that silently does not apply.
#
# `pnpm dev` writes this for you. Without it the worker is built, served over
# HTTPS and reachable from the phone — and nothing ever registers it, which
# looks identical to a broken PWA. The console says so from v0.1.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ENABLE_SW="1"

# ── Push notifications ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Three values, and you do not have to set them here at all: Settings →
# Notifications has a Generate keys button that creates the pair and stores it
# in the database, with the private half encrypted at rest. Use these only if
# you would rather configuration lived in the environment.
#
# The DATABASE WINS. Every setting resolves database first, environment second
# — the environment is a seed for an instance that has never been configured,
# not an override. So generating a pair in Settings replaces whatever is here.
#
# To generate them yourself:  pnpm push:keys mailto:you@yourdomain.com
#
# Do NOT uncomment these lines without replacing the values. The placeholders
# below are deliberately not ellipses any more: an earlier version used `…`,
# and uncommenting it made the public key the single character U+2026 — a
# non-empty string, so the app reported itself configured, and the browser
# failed inside atob() with a message about the Latin1 range that mentioned
# neither VAPID nor this file. A malformed key is now caught on read and
# reported as unconfigured, but a placeholder that cannot be mistaken for a
# value is the better half of the fix.
#
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY   the uncompressed P-256 point, 65 bytes base64url (87 chars).
#                    Public: it is handed to every browser that subscribes.
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY  the raw 32-byte scalar, base64url (43 chars). Secret.
# VAPID_SUBJECT      a mailto: address or https: URL a push service can use to
#                    reach whoever runs this instance. Required by RFC 8292; a
#                    bare email address is rejected. Never shown to recipients.
#
# Generate the pair ONCE. A browser binds each subscription to the public key it
# was created with, so replacing it does not migrate those subscriptions and
# does not error — every device that had notifications on silently stops
# receiving them. Keep a copy of the private key. See docs/PWA.md.
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY="REPLACE_WITH_87_CHAR_BASE64URL_PUBLIC_KEY"
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY="REPLACE_WITH_43_CHAR_BASE64URL_PRIVATE_KEY"
# VAPID_SUBJECT="mailto:you@yourdomain.com"

# ── Resend ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The key needs BOTH sending and receiving permission. A send-only key produces
# an inbox that is silently always empty.
# RESEND_API_KEY="re_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
# RESEND_DOMAIN="mail.example.com"
# RESEND_FROM_EMAIL="hello@mail.example.com"
# RESEND_FROM_NAME="Acme"
#
# From the webhook's page in Resend. Starts with whsec_. Without it the webhook
# endpoint rejects everything — which is correct: an unauthenticated endpoint
# would let anyone forge a bounce and suppress an address on your instance.
# RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET="whsec_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"

# ── Cloudinary (recommended, especially on Vercel) ───────────────────────────
# Hosts your logo and favicon on a CDN. Without it they are stored in your
# database and served by the app — which works, but your logo is embedded in
# every campaign, so each recipient's mail client fetches it through a
# serverless function. A 50,000-person send turns one image into tens of
# thousands of invocations.
#
# All three or none: a partial configuration fails at upload time with an
# opaque signature error rather than falling back.
# CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME="your-cloud-name"
# CLOUDINARY_API_KEY="123456789012345"
# CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET="your-api-secret"
# CLOUDINARY_FOLDER="sendstack"

# ── Redis (optional feature, not just an optional variable) ──────────────────
# Makes new replies appear instantly instead of on refresh. Resend has no
# streaming API, so inbound mail arrives by webhook on whichever instance the
# platform picks — Redis relays it to the browser holding your inbox open.
# Skip it entirely and everything still works correctly.
#
# The scheme decides which client is used:
#
#   redis://  or  rediss://   a normal Redis server. No token — any credentials
#                             go in the URL. Works with `docker compose up -d`,
#                             Redis Cloud, Railway, Fly, or your own box.
#   https://                  Upstash's REST API, which also needs a token.
#                             Prefer this on serverless: a redis:// connection
#                             has to be re-established on every cold start.
#
# REDIS_URL is honoured too, which is what Railway, Fly and Redis Cloud set for
# you automatically.
# REDIS_URL="redis://localhost:6379"
#
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL="https://your-db.upstash.io"
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN="your-upstash-rest-token"

# ── Inngest ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local development needs neither: `pnpm dev` starts the Inngest dev server and
# it discovers this app automatically. In production without these, campaigns
# queue but never send — nothing errors, they just sit there.
# INNGEST_EVENT_KEY=""
# INNGEST_SIGNING_KEY=""
# INNGEST_DEV=1
